1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,407
Your Java app will not start. The error is long, and scary. Most people panic.

2
00:00:05,957 --> 00:00:08,223
But a Java failure is a short story.

3
00:00:08,223 --> 00:00:09,735
The first line names it.

4
00:00:09,735 --> 00:00:12,442
And the cause is almost always one of four.

5
00:00:12,992 --> 00:00:15,956
Today you meet all four: the wrong Java version,

6
00:00:15,956 --> 00:00:16,820
out of memory,

7
00:00:16,820 --> 00:00:18,178
a port already in use,

8
00:00:18,178 --> 00:00:19,413
and a missing class.

9
00:00:19,963 --> 00:00:22,139
You read each error, name it, and fix it.

10
00:00:24,189 --> 00:00:26,173
Four failures, four first lines.

11
00:00:26,723 --> 00:00:26,985
One.

12
00:00:26,985 --> 00:00:28,691
Unsupported class version.

13
00:00:28,691 --> 00:00:32,626
The class was built by a newer Java than the one running it.

14
00:00:32,626 --> 00:00:34,659
Fix: rebuild it with this Java.

15
00:00:35,209 --> 00:00:35,500
Two.

16
00:00:35,500 --> 00:00:36,520
Out of memory,

17
00:00:36,520 --> 00:00:37,685
Java heap space.

18
00:00:37,685 --> 00:00:40,526
The program needed more than its limit.

19
00:00:40,526 --> 00:00:43,148
Fix: a bigger limit with dash X M X,

20
00:00:43,148 --> 00:00:44,532
or less memory use.

21
00:00:45,082 --> 00:00:45,485
Three.

22
00:00:45,485 --> 00:00:47,030
Address already in use,

23
00:00:47,030 --> 00:00:48,173
a bind exception.

24
00:00:48,173 --> 00:00:50,794
Another program already owns that port.

25
00:00:50,794 --> 00:00:52,070
Fix: free the port,

26
00:00:52,070 --> 00:00:53,146
or pick another.

27
00:00:53,696 --> 00:00:54,050
Four.

28
00:00:54,050 --> 00:00:55,894
No class definition found.

29
00:00:55,894 --> 00:00:59,511
A class the program needs is not on the class path.

30
00:00:59,511 --> 00:01:01,568
Fix: put its jar on dash C P.

31
00:01:02,118 --> 00:01:04,614
Read the first line. It names the problem.

32
00:01:06,664 --> 00:01:09,651
Go to the shop folder. Type: CD, shop.

33
00:01:10,201 --> 00:01:15,854
Failure one. Type: sh run-version.sh

34
00:01:15,984 --> 00:01:22,107
Unsupported class version. Built as version sixty-nine. This Java knows up to sixty-five.

35
00:01:22,657 --> 00:01:27,777
Which Java is running? Type: java --version | head -1

36
00:01:27,907 --> 00:01:31,619
Java twenty-one. It runs class versions up to sixty-five.

37
00:01:32,169 --> 00:01:41,897
Which version is the class? Type: od -An -tu1 -j7 -N1 ver/classes/cloudcart/Greeter.class

38
00:01:42,027 --> 00:01:42,766
Sixty-nine.

39
00:01:42,766 --> 00:01:44,446
That is Java twenty-five.

40
00:01:44,446 --> 00:01:47,403
In this lab I raised that number on purpose,

41
00:01:47,403 --> 00:01:50,091
because this computer has only one Java.

42
00:01:50,641 --> 00:01:53,928
The fix: rebuild it from source with this Java.

43
00:01:53,928 --> 00:01:57,915
Type: javac -d ver/classes ver/src/cloudcart/Greeter.java

44
00:01:58,045 --> 00:02:02,184
Run it again. Type: sh run-version.sh

45
00:02:02,314 --> 00:02:04,618
O K. Built for the Java that runs it.

46
00:02:05,168 --> 00:02:09,264
Failure two. Type: sh run-memory.sh

47
00:02:09,394 --> 00:02:14,045
Out of memory error. Java heap space. The program wanted more than its limit.

48
00:02:14,595 --> 00:02:19,331
What is the limit? Type: grep Xmx run-memory.sh

49
00:02:19,461 --> 00:02:24,794
Dash X M X sixteen M. Sixteen megabytes. This program needs sixty-four.

50
00:02:25,344 --> 00:02:30,208
Raise it with sed. Type: sed -i 's/-Xmx16m/-Xmx256m/' run-memory.sh

51
00:02:30,338 --> 00:02:34,455
Run it again. Type: sh run-memory.sh

52
00:02:34,585 --> 00:02:37,380
Two failures fixed, each by reading one line.

53
00:02:39,430 --> 00:02:42,417
Go to the shop folder. Type: CD, shop.

54
00:02:42,967 --> 00:02:52,289
Failure three. Type: sh run-port.sh 2>&1 | head -4

55
00:02:52,419 --> 00:02:57,582
The first server got the port. The second hit a bind exception. Address already in use.

56
00:02:58,132 --> 00:03:02,655
Who asked for what? Type: grep Server run-port.sh

57
00:03:02,785 --> 00:03:06,838
Both servers ask for port eighteen oh eight three. Only one can have it.

58
00:03:07,388 --> 00:03:09,410
Give the second one its own port.

59
00:03:09,410 --> 00:03:13,148
Type: sed -i 's/Server 18083 ||/Server 18084 ||/' run-port.sh

60
00:03:13,278 --> 00:03:17,225
Run it again. Type: sh run-port.sh

61
00:03:17,355 --> 00:03:19,381
Two servers, two ports. Fixed.

62
00:03:19,931 --> 00:03:29,318
Failure four. Type: sh run-classpath.sh 2>&1 | head -4

63
00:03:29,448 --> 00:03:30,993
No class definition found.

64
00:03:30,993 --> 00:03:32,895
The tool needs the helper class,

65
00:03:32,895 --> 00:03:35,272
and the helper is not on the class path.

66
00:03:35,822 --> 00:03:41,241
What class path does it use? Type: grep java run-classpath.sh

67
00:03:41,371 --> 00:03:43,888
Only the classes folder. Where is the helper?

68
00:03:44,438 --> 00:03:48,065
Look in the lib folder. Type: ls cp/lib

69
00:03:48,195 --> 00:03:52,077
Helper dot jar. It exists. It is just not on the class path.

70
00:03:52,627 --> 00:03:53,028
Add it.

71
00:03:53,028 --> 00:03:57,833
Type: sed -i 's|-cp cp/classes |-cp cp/classes:cp/lib/helper.jar |' run-classpath.sh

72
00:03:59,963 --> 00:04:04,208
Run it again. Type: sh run-classpath.sh

73
00:04:04,338 --> 00:04:07,730
Four failures. Four first lines. Four small fixes.

74
00:04:09,780 --> 00:04:11,636
How to diagnose, in this order.

75
00:04:12,186 --> 00:04:15,279
First, read the first error line. It names the failure.

76
00:04:15,829 --> 00:04:20,715
Second, find the cause. The version, the limit, the owner of the port, or the class path.

77
00:04:21,265 --> 00:04:23,441
Third, change one thing, and run again.

78
00:04:23,991 --> 00:04:26,615
One change at a time. Then you know what fixed it.

79
00:04:28,665 --> 00:04:29,518
Your lab task.

80
00:04:29,518 --> 00:04:32,929
Lab eighty-three gives you four broken launcher scripts.

81
00:04:32,929 --> 00:04:34,574
Lab check judges your work.

82
00:04:35,124 --> 00:04:37,218
It lives at git hub dot com,

83
00:04:37,218 --> 00:04:38,713
slash it dash waves,

84
00:04:38,713 --> 00:04:40,432
slash dev ops dash A I,

85
00:04:40,432 --> 00:04:41,255
in academy,

86
00:04:41,255 --> 00:04:41,554
lab,

87
00:04:41,554 --> 00:04:43,423
lab eighty-three dot S H.

88
00:04:43,973 --> 00:04:47,450
One: run dash version. Rebuild the class with this Java.

89
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:53,035
Two: run dash memory. Raise the heap limit to two hundred fifty-six megabytes.

90
00:04:53,585 --> 00:04:57,894
Three: run dash port. Give the second server port eighteen oh eight four.

91
00:04:58,444 --> 00:05:02,284
Four: run dash class path. Put the helper jar on the class path.

92
00:05:02,834 --> 00:05:07,677
Five: diag dot text. Four lines, one exception name for each failure.

93
00:05:08,227 --> 00:05:08,751
Install,

94
00:05:08,751 --> 00:05:09,537
then lab up,

95
00:05:09,537 --> 00:05:10,192
lab check,

96
00:05:10,192 --> 00:05:11,044
and lab down,

97
00:05:11,044 --> 00:05:11,830
like before.

98
00:05:11,830 --> 00:05:14,712
The shortcut file is shortcuts eighty-three.

99
00:05:16,762 --> 00:05:18,831
Pass or fail. You need all six.

100
00:05:19,381 --> 00:05:21,195
Five pass lines in lab check.

101
00:05:21,745 --> 00:05:24,305
You can name each exception from its first line.

102
00:05:24,855 --> 00:05:26,647
You can explain a version mismatch.

103
00:05:27,197 --> 00:05:28,818
You can explain a port in use.

104
00:05:29,368 --> 00:05:31,544
You can explain a missing class path entry.

105
00:05:32,094 --> 00:05:34,014
lab down, nothing left behind.

106
00:05:34,564 --> 00:05:35,865
To pass, all six.

107
00:05:37,915 --> 00:05:40,752
Your turn, in your own Ubuntu virtual machine.

108
00:05:41,302 --> 00:05:43,393
One: lab up, and read the task file.

109
00:05:43,943 --> 00:05:47,314
Two: run each script, and read only the first error line.

110
00:05:47,864 --> 00:05:49,997
Three: fix the version and the memory.

111
00:05:50,547 --> 00:05:52,659
Four: fix the port and the class path.

112
00:05:53,209 --> 00:05:55,918
Five: write diag dot text, and lab check.

113
00:05:56,468 --> 00:05:59,050
Six: lab down, and prove nothing is left.

114
00:05:59,600 --> 00:06:01,434
Pause the video, and begin now.

115
00:06:16,984 --> 00:06:17,859
Quick check.

116
00:06:18,409 --> 00:06:21,545
Which exception means a class was built by a newer Java?

117
00:06:22,095 --> 00:06:23,823
What does dash X M X set?

118
00:06:24,373 --> 00:06:26,229
What does address already in use mean?

119
00:06:26,779 --> 00:06:29,125
What does no class definition found point to?

120
00:06:29,675 --> 00:06:33,323
